A1. This can raise an appetite and a check. ~ CANAPE
A6. The only thing chorus girls don't make any great display of. ~ BRAINS
A11. These will have to last stock brokers another season. ~ OVERCOATS
A13. This is the biggest part of a cur. ~ UR
A15. To neat up. ~ ARRANGE
A16. The first thing you see in Canada. ~ CA
A17. A couple of words from Mussolini. ~ IAM
A19. The way a cowboy moves. ~ TOTES
A20. Nathan's favorite frying device. ~ PAN
A21. Horse-mackerel, or, if you please, a carangoid fish. ~ SCAD
A23. The things you hear tell of in court. ~ RES
A24. Victorian binge. ~ LARK
A25. The spooky canal. ~ EERIE
A27. This gets 'em coming and going. ~ DEPOT
A28. A clown shooter. ~ HORSEPISTOL
A29. These will mind your business. ~ BOOKS
A30. Lofty singers. ~ CHOIR
A32. Ocean flyers. ~ ERNS
A33. One who lives on other people's troubles. ~ LLD
A35. This is a slave to puzzlers. ~ ESNE
A36. Medieval transport. ~ ASS
A37. Export place for fat tenors. ~ MILAN
A39. This one can wriggle out of anything. ~ EEL
A40. The beginning of necking. ~ NE
A41. The kind of people you never find at home. ~ NOMADIC
A43. Encouragement from a senorita. ~ SI
A44. Indigent yachtsmen travel in these. ~ STEERAGES
A46. With all her charm Eve wasn't this. ~ DRESSY
A47. Ideal husbands for shop girls. ~ SHEIKS

D1. A long trip by water prescribed for people who are in need of a steady liquid diet. ~ CRUISE
D2. Encouragement from an American girl. ~ NO
D3. The topaz-humming bird. ~ AVA
D4. Out in Kansas they call this sassy. ~ PERT
D5. Something it is not in the least possible for the Christian Science Shortstop to commit. ~ ERROR
D6. Weather forecasters. ~ BONES
D7. What they say the well-dressed Wall Street speculator will wear for the coming season. ~ RAGS
D8. Picked something up at the fountain. ~ ATE
D9. If you fill this in properly you'll land right in the middle of prison. ~ IS
D10. Hurt Willie's feelings (simp. sp.) ~ SPANKT
D12. An upholstered worm. ~ CATERPILLAR
D14. This also runs with your money. ~ RACEHORSE
D16. This is what every Tom, Dick and Harriet in the country were singing and crying for recently. ~ CAROLINES
D18. Eleven Staggs from Chicago. ~ MAROONS
D20. The result of an Indian love lyric. ~ PAPOOSE
D22. Stickers. ~ DIRKS
D24. Grecian Weary River. ~ LETHE
D26. The end of sadness. ~ ESS
D27. A band that has gotten together for the sole purpose of making money following the horses. ~ DSC
D29. They didn't dine the visiting celebrity who came to Boston; they did this to him. ~ BEANED
D31. Wornout husbands. ~ RELICS
D33. A sour Cockney. ~ LIMEY
D34. Sugar baby's cry at twilight (pl.) ~ DADAS
D37. Belonging to that Levy fellow. ~ MOES
D38. Near. ~ NIGH
D41. Nubian's Temperance Society, initially. ~ NTS
D42. You'll get a hiss out of this. ~ CEE
D44. Pretty nearly set. ~ SE
D45. Encouragement from an Italian girl—and we also consider it the beginning of sin. ~ SI


